# 🪟 Windows Theory

# Windows Desktop

The Desktop is the main workspace in Windows where users access applications, folders, files, and shortcuts.

Components:

- Desktop Icons
- Taskbar
- Start Menu
- Notification Area
- Search Bar

---

# Start Menu

The Start Menu is used to:

- Search applications
- Open Settings
- Shut down or restart the computer
- Access installed programs

---

# Taskbar

The Taskbar contains:

- Start Button
- Running Applications
- Search
- File Explorer
- Network Status
- Volume
- Date & Time
- Notification Icons

---

# File Explorer

File Explorer is the primary file management tool in Windows.

Functions:

- Create folders
- Copy files
- Move files
- Rename files
- Delete files
- Search files
- Access local and network drives

Shortcut:

```text
Windows + E
```

---

# This PC

"This PC" displays:

- Local drives
- External USB drives
- CD/DVD drives (if available)
- Network locations
- Storage information

---

# Recycle Bin

The Recycle Bin temporarily stores deleted files and folders.

Functions:

- Recover deleted files
- Permanently delete files
- Empty the Recycle Bin

---

# Settings vs Control Panel

## Settings

Modern Windows configuration interface.

Common tasks:

- Windows Update
- Display settings
- Network settings
- Bluetooth
- Personalization

---

## Control Panel

Legacy Windows configuration tool.

Common tasks:

- Programs and Features
- Administrative Tools
- Device configuration
- User Accounts
- Network configuration

---

# Task Manager

Shortcut:

```text
Ctrl + Shift + Esc
```

Task Manager is used to:

- View running applications
- Monitor CPU usage
- Monitor Memory usage
- End unresponsive tasks
- Restart Windows Explorer
- Monitor system performance

